THE ROW WAS ADDED THIS ROUND (item 2) AND ITS OWN COMMENT STATES THE CONTRACT IT BREAKS: 'CONVERGED MEANS THE CORPUS RAN OUT OF PAIRS, and it is NOT the size >= target break above. Setting it there too would say a build that reached its target had nothing left to mint, which is the opposite claim.' The break it hangs on, `if minted == 0:`, has THREE causes and the row can only distinguish one:
  (a) no candidate reached tok.min_pair -- convergence, what the row claims;
  (b) every candidate above min_pair was refused by Vocabulary._add for `len(seq) > self.max_bytes`;
  (c) every candidate was already in seq2id.
On (b) and (c) the build stalls far below target with a full tally and the ledger says the corpus is exhausted. THIS IS NOT A HYPOTHETICAL CONFIGURATION -- tok/levers.py::max_bytes's own comment records it: 'measured max_tok=6 vmax=4000 -> stalled at 658/4000 with 1866 pairs still above min_pair (83.5% dead) (tokenizer.py:318-324). A ceiling that stops a burst instead of skipping a candidate turns a length preference into a vocabulary cap nobody asked for.' mint_burst's docstring states the same rule for the ONLINE path ('A candidate refused for max_bytes or for already existing is SKIPPED, not returned as "nothing left to mint"'). The BUILD loop still has that hole -- which is PRE-EXISTING, the `break` predates this round -- and what this round added is a counter that now prints a false cause over it. A Gate printing a false equation is worse than one printing nothing, and this is that, applied to a count.
MEASURED (my script, outside the repo; 6000 random words from a 20-word list, one area, TOK_BUILD_BYTES=200000, TOK_SEED_VOCAB=2000, TOK_BUILD_PASSES=6, TOK_MIN_PAIR at its default 50):
  TOK_MAX_BYTES=6  -> counters {tok.build_pass: 4, tok.build_mint: 210, tok.build_converged: 1}, size 466 of target 2000, and re-tallying the build sample against the finished vocabulary leaves NINE pairs at or above min_pair, top b'charlie ' at 183 occurrences (8 bytes > 6, refused for length alone).
  TOK_MAX_BYTES=2  -> {tok.build_pass: 2, tok.build_mint: 94, tok.build_converged: 1}, size 350 of 2000, EIGHTY-NINE pairs still >= 50, top b'char' at 183.
  TOK_MAX_BYTES=16 (the shipped default, same corpus) -> {build_pass: 3, build_mint: 15, build_converged: 1} with ZERO pairs remaining above min_pair -- so at the default the row is TRUE, which is exactly why this will not be noticed.
THE FIX, no functionality removed. In build_vocabulary's mint loop, separate the refusal from the exhaustion:

